Conclusion and Benchmark Analysis

The benchmark results from the Campus Graph demonstrate the performance advantages of the A* search algorithm over Uniform Cost Search (UCS). By leveraging the custom 3D heuristic, A* consistently expanded significantly fewer nodes and achieved faster execution times across most standard tasks, while still guaranteeing the identical optimal path cost. This validates the admissibility and efficiency of the heuristic design.

The execution of the custom scenarios confirms the successful implementation of dynamic graph filtering based on user profiles. The pathfinding engine accurately enforced the "wheelchair" constraints by automatically bypassing inaccessible routes (such as stairs) to find alternative paths. Furthermore, when presented with a complete physical impasse, the system correctly aborted the search and triggered the automated call guidance flag, ensuring safety and compliance with the project's accessibility requirements.

Furthermore, Scenario 4 confirms dynamic consistency; across 15 complex routing queries on a 30-node graph, both UCS and A* systematically produced identical optimal costs, confirming algorithmic accuracy regardless of the traversal pattern. The Massive Graph Stress Test further highlighted the engine's robust memory management and speed, seamlessly processing a 10,000-node grid and demonstrating the framework's capability to scale efficiently.

Finally, the edge case evaluations prove the overall robustness of the search architecture. The algorithms safely handled zero-cost edges without entering infinite expansion loops, resolved self-loops (where the start node equals the goal node) instantly with zero cost, and managed total blockades and missing nodes by gracefully returning a failed search state rather than crashing. These results confirm that the pathfinding engine is highly stable, deterministic, and fully capable of handling complex navigation queries on both micro and macro scales.